rows.Guidance:
Encourage the mother to continue breastfeeding. Teach correct positioning and attachment. Reassess after two feeds (or 1 day). If not better, teach the mother how to express breast-milk from the affected breast and feed the infant using a cup, and continue breastfeeding on the healthy side. Keep the mother and infant together in bed or within easy reach. Do not separate them. Encourage breastfeeding on demand, day and night, as long as the infant wants it.

An infant needs to feed day and night, eight or more times in 24 hours from birth. Only on the first day may an infant born full-term sleep many hours after a good feed. Show the mother how to hold her infant. She should: 
→ make sure the infant’s head and body are in a straight line; 
→ make sure the infant is facing the breast and the infant’s nose is opposite her nipple; 
→ hold the infant’s body close to her body 
→ support the infant’s whole body, not just the neck and shoulders. 

Show the mother how to help her infant to attach. She should: 
→ touch her infant’s lips with her nipple; 
→ wait until her infant’s mouth is opened wide; 
→ move her infant quickly onto her breast, aiming the infant’s lower lip well below the nipple. 

Look for signs of good attachment, such as: 
→ more of the areola visible above the infant’s mouth; 
→ mouth wide open; 
→ lower lip turned outwards; 
→ infant’s chin touching the breast. 

Look for signs of effective suckling (i.e. slow, deep sucks, sometimes pausing). If the attachment or suckling is not good, try again. Then, reassess.

